  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled with status `ok`. One gap: I couldn't check the exact response codes in the data, because the API key got a 401 when I tried to query Elasticsearch directly. The 5xx filter is written to cover that (see the rule section below).\n\n**1. Fields.** The data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The main fields are:\n- `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text, with a `response.keyword` version)\n- `bytes`, `clientip`, `url`, `request`, `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest` (country codes)\n- `machine.os`, `agent`, `host`, `referer`, `tags`\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-4673be07 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-4673be07-web-logs-overview-e8e24dd4a5b4`, at `$KIBANA_URL/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4673be07-web-logs-overview-e8e24dd4a5b4`). It has 7 panels:\n- **Total requests:** a single number.\n- **Unique visitors:** a single number, counting distinct `clientip`.\n- **Average bytes per request:** a single number.\n- **Requests over time by response code:** a bar chart over time.\n- **Response code share:** a pie chart.\n- **Top requested URLs:** a table with request count and total bytes per URL.\n- **Requests by destination country:** a region map.\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-4673be07 5xx spike\"** (id `7d1220da-15e7-4533-b91f-88bd6d174293`).\n- It runs every minute on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field.\n- It fires when more than 10 matching documents arrive within 5 minutes.\n- A request counts as 5xx if `response.keyword` starts with \"5\". Because of that prefix, the filter matches whichever 5xx codes the data contains, not just one. The rule has no notification action, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana.",
